It was observed that Pb was consumed most through turmeric powder in both the category and lowest for
cumin powder in the branded category and coriander powder for the nonbranded category. The
total daily intake of the studied metal through the consumption of different spices was higher than the
maximum tolerable intake (MTDI) only in Red Pepper and Turmeric Powder for Nonbranded items (0.21
g) [16], indicating that these spices might pose a risk to consumers in the study area of Bangladesh.
Since the EDI of the studied metals was higher than the MTDI, periodic surveillance will be necessary to
set up regulatory norms for the dietary intake of spices, especially for nonbranded items. Because
Bangladeshi people are taking a high amount of spice compared to other countries, most of the people
are using the studied spice every day in a very high amount for food preparation. Reported that
Bangladeshi people average estimated daily intake approximately 66 g of spices [16], but the average
daily intake of common spices per person has been estimated as 4.0 g in the USA, 0.5 g in Europe and 1.0
g in New Zealand and 14.7 g in South Asia [34].



The noncancer risk was estimated by associating an exposure level over a definite time period with a
reference amount resulting for an analogous exposure period. The noncancer risk has been described by
a hazard quotient (HQ). The target hazard quotient (THQ) of Pb consumption by average Bangladeshi
adults was calculated using equation no (3), and the level of THQ is presented in Table 3. The THQ
values of lead were less than 1, which suggests that people would be totally free from noncancer risk if
they consumed heavy metals through the consumption of the studied spices.




